/*
    Upgrade Tiers - raise each item to the highest tier it actually supports

    In the base game a store rerolls the tiered variants of what it stocks each
    time it is reopened, using the buyer's level, and the result may go up, go
    down, or stay the same. This does the same for items already in the party's
    possession, but keeps the result only when the tier rises.

    Which tiers an item supports
    ---------------------------
    Each item carries a MaterialProgress row, and the material table (199) holds that
    row's Material1 to Material15 columns. The row is the authority on what the item
    may become: a Darkspawn Dagger's row stops at Steel, so nothing will ever make it
    Dragonbone. A hardcoded list of materials ignored the row and produced items at
    tiers that do not exist for them.

    The target is the best the vendor could stock for that item at the buyer's level.
    The row's columns are slots, not tiers. What a player calls a tier is the material's
    own rank: Iron/Grey Iron/Steel/Veridium/Red Steel/Silverite/Dragonbone for metal,
    Elm/Ash/Yew/Whitewood/Ironbark/Sylvanwood/Dragonthorn for wood, and the hide ladder
    for leather. The material types table (89) does not carry that rank for wood or
    leather, so the ranks are stated here as per family ladders of material ids,
    checked against all 1016 item templates.

    The target is the best tier the store would stock for a buyer of this level:

        level  1-2  ->  2      level  9-10 ->  6
        level  3-4  ->  3      level 11-13 ->  7
        level  5-6  ->  4      level 14-16 ->  8
        level  7-8  ->  5      level 17+   ->  9

    Tier 7 at level 12 matches what a store was seen offering; the other bands are
    fitted rather than confirmed, so they are worth checking at a second level. The
    whole row is then scanned and the best material whose tier is at or below that
    target is applied.

    A row is a family, not an item. Of the 1016 item templates only 54 distinct rows
    are in use, and one row alone covers 258 templates; just 12 rows belong to a single
    item. So a row states what a *family* scales through, not that each member exists at
    every step. That is why the Darkspawn Dagger stopping at Steel is correct - its whole
    family ends there - and why a generous family row must not be applied to a named
    piece.

    Named items are skipped: they are hand placed rather than generated by a store, so
    the game never rerolls their material. The Helm of Honnleath belongs to a family
    whose row reaches Red Steel while the helm itself was authored in Grey Iron, and
    following the row put it at a tier it was never made in.

    Items the game cannot name are skipped as well. A creature's built-in attack has a
    real progression row and can sit in the party inventory - the Mabari's bite is one -
    and raising its tier makes a companion hit harder than the designers authored, with
    nothing in the UI to show it. Its name reference resolves to no string at all, which
    is also why it once printed as a bare "2->7".

    Only weapons, shields and armour are raised. The base item table records what each
    kind of item is, and anything else - a ring, a gift, a quest trinket that can only be
    sold - is misc, so it is left alone however good its progression row looks.

    Runes
    -----
    An item's runes are dropped when its material changes. The engine does expose
    AddItemSubItem (822) and RemoveItemSubItem (823), but neither is declared in
    script.ldf and the compiler refuses to bind to an id it cannot resolve, so
    restoring them is out of reach from a script. The slot capability variable that
    other upgrade mods overwrite (ITEM_RUNE_ENABLED) is never written here.

    Reading the result
    ------------------
    Floaties are the only channel that renders in the retail build; the on-screen
    debug text and the GUI log window both produce nothing. The whole run is collected
    and shown once, as a single floaty. Identical items collapse into one line with a
    count, because a stash holds many copies of the same few things:

        4x Magic Staff 1->3
        2x Magic Staff 2->3
        1x Enchanter's Staff 3->7
        1x Dalish Bow 5->7

    Each line reads as: how many, the item's name, the tier it was on, the tier it
    moved to. Entries keep first-seen order, which follows inventory order, so like
    items usually arrive together rather than being sorted.

    Nothing else is printed. A run with no changes says "nothing to upgrade", because
    silence is otherwise indistinguishable from a script that never ran.

    Usage
    -----
        runscript upgrade_tiers          the tier your level earns
        runscript upgrade_tiers 9        force a target tier (1-9)
        runscript upgrade_tiers -        dry run at the tier your level earns
        runscript upgrade_tiers -9       dry run at a forced tier
        runscript upgrade_tiers c3       raise Shale's crystals to quality 3 only
        runscript upgrade_tiers -c5      dry run of the same

    A dry run performs the same sweep and prints the same list, but never calls
    SetItemMaterialType. Since it changes nothing it can be repeated freely, which is
    the way to see the effect of a target tier before committing to it. The leading
    minus is what selects it, and is read from the text, so "-" on its own works.

    Crystals are excluded from every mode but their own. Their quality is not a material
    but a separate template per step, so raising one means creating the better crystal
    and destroying the old item - which cannot be done in place, and which leaves the
    replacement in the inventory rather than in the slot the old one occupied.

    A single run covers the whole inventory. The engine's own per-script instruction
    limit used to stop this script partway, silently and with no error, which is what
    made earlier versions appear to do nothing; it has far more than enough headroom
    now, so the work no longer has to be spread over repeated invocations.

        Upgraded 24 items to tier 7
        All done

    The shared party stash is read once, because that is what it is: one inventory that
    every party member reports. Reading it per creature returned the same items again and
    again and reported each change once per creature. Worn gear is the only thing that
    differs per creature, so companions are swept with GET_ITEMS_OPTION_EQUIPPED alone.
*/

#include "log_h"
#include "utility_h"
#include "wrappers_h"
#include "events_h"
#include "2da_constants_h"


// The engine used to stop any script at 131072 VM instructions, silently, which is
// why this script once had to change only a handful of items per run and be
// re-invoked. DAFix raises that cap, so a single run now covers the whole inventory
// and this is only a safety valve against a runaway loop. It is far above any
// inventory this game can produce.
const int MAX_UPDATES = 10000;

const string BUILD = "ut74";

int g_nChanged = 0;

// A dry run reports what would change and writes nothing, so a run can be checked
// before it touches the save.
int g_bDryRun = FALSE;

// Every item that would change is appended here as one line, and the duplicates are
// collapsed when the report is built. A string rather than an array on purpose: no
// shipped DAO script declares an array at file scope, and a scalar keeps this portable.
string g_sUpdated = "";

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- progression lookups

// The engine knows which materials an item may become: each item carries a
// MaterialProgress row, and the material table (199) holds that row's Material1 to
// Material15 columns. Reading the row respects an item's own ceiling, so a Darkspawn
// Dagger that stops at Steel stays at Steel.
//
// The row alone is not enough. Tier numbers cannot be read from the material types
// table (89) for every family: wood and leather materials return nothing there, which
// silently skipped every bow, crossbow and piece of light armour. The ladders below are
// the material ids per family, checked against all 1016 item templates; no material
// appears in two ladders. They name the same tiers the wiki does, so Dragonbone,
// Dragonthorn and Drakeskin are all tier 7.
const int MAT_COLUMNS = 15;

// Material tiers run 1 to 9. The argument to the command is a tier, not a column.
const int MAX_TIER = 9;

int MaterialAtColumn(int nProgression, int nColumn)
{
    if (nProgression <= 0 || nColumn < 1 || nColumn > MAT_COLUMNS)
    {
        return 0;
    }
    return GetM2DAInt(TABLE_MATERIAL, "Material" + ToString(nColumn), nProgression);
}


// The rank of a material, 1 to 9, across every family. The material types table (89)
// does not carry this for wood or leather, so the ranks are stated here: Iron and Elm
// and Rough Hide are all tier 1, and Dragonbone and Dragonthorn and Drakeskin are all
// tier 7. This is used only to read a tier; the progression row still decides which
// materials an item may become.
int TierOfMaterial(int nMaterial)
{
    switch (nMaterial)
    {
        case 40: case 1: case 60: case 47: case 33: case 79:
            return 1;
        case 41: case 2: case 28: case 48: case 34: case 9:
            return 2;
        case 43: case 8: case 29: case 49: case 35: case 11:
            return 3;
        case 42: case 4: case 30: case 81: case 36: case 15:
            return 4;
        case 44: case 5: case 31: case 50: case 37: case 13:
            return 5;
        case 45: case 3: case 17: case 51: case 38: case 7:
            return 6;
        case 46: case 6: case 32: case 52: case 39: case 10:
            return 7;

        case 20050: case 20010: case 20030: case 20060: case 20040: case 20020:
            return 8;
        case 20051: case 20011: case 20031: case 20061: case 20041: case 20021:
            return 9;
    }
    return 0;
}

// The tier the store stocks for a buyer of this level. Calibrated from observed
// vendor behaviour: at level 11-12 the store offers Dragonbone, which is tier 7. A
// plain (level/2)+1 gives 6 there, one short, so the bands are stated explicitly.
// The creature level is printed in the diagnostics so this can be checked against the
// vendor in one run.
int TierForLevel(int nLevel)
{
    if (nLevel <= 2) { return 2; }
    if (nLevel <= 4) { return 3; }
    if (nLevel <= 6) { return 4; }
    if (nLevel <= 8) { return 5; }
    if (nLevel <= 10) { return 6; }
    if (nLevel <= 13) { return 7; }
    if (nLevel <= 16) { return 8; }
    return 9;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- item work

int CreatureLevel(object oCreature)
{
    return FloatToInt(GetCreatureProperty(oCreature, PROPERTY_SIMPLE_LEVEL));
}

// Adds one item to the report. Duplicates are collapsed later, by BuildReport.
void Record(string sKey)
{
    g_sUpdated = g_sUpdated + sKey + "\n";
}

// Collapses "name old->new" repeats into "Nx name old->new".
//
// The duplicates cannot be counted as they arrive without an array, and no shipped DAO
// script declares one at file scope, so the lines are grouped here instead: take the
// first line, walk the rest counting matches and keeping the non-matches, then repeat
// on what is left. A stash holds many copies of a few items, so this runs a handful of
// passes over a short string rather than one pass per distinct item.
string BuildReport(string sAll)
{
    string sRemaining = sAll;
    string sReport = "";
    while (sRemaining != "")
    {
        int nLength = GetStringLength(sRemaining);
        int nEnd = FindSubString(sRemaining, "\n", 0);
        if (nEnd < 0)
        {
            nEnd = nLength;
        }
        string sLine = SubString(sRemaining, 0, nEnd);

        string sRest = "";
        int nCount = 1;
        int nPos = nEnd + 1;
        while (nPos < nLength)
        {
            int nNext = FindSubString(sRemaining, "\n", nPos);
            if (nNext < 0)
            {
                nNext = nLength;
            }
            string sCurrent = SubString(sRemaining, nPos, nNext - nPos);
            if (sCurrent == sLine)
            {
                nCount = nCount + 1;
            }
            else if (sCurrent != "")
            {
                sRest = sRest + sCurrent + "\n";
            }
            nPos = nNext + 1;
        }

        sReport = sReport + IntToString(nCount) + "x " + sLine + "\n";
        sRemaining = sRest;
    }
    return sReport;
}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------- Shale's crystals

// A crystal's quality is not a material - it is a separate template for each of the
// five steps, so raising one means creating the better template and removing the old
// item. That is the only upgrade in this script that cannot be done in place, and it is
// why crystals are handled by their own mode instead of being swept with everything else:
// a normal run leaves them alone entirely.
//
// The tag is the only handle on quality: "shl_arm_fir_00" is a Large Chipped Fire Crystal,
// shl_arm_ or shl_wpn_ the kind, fir/ice/lit/nat/spi the element, and the last two digits
// the quality from 00 (Chipped) to 04 (Brilliant).

int CrystalIndex(object oItem)
{
    string sTag = GetTag(oItem);
    if (GetStringLength(sTag) != 14)
    {
        return -1;
    }
    if (SubString(sTag, 0, 4) != "shl_")
    {
        return -1;
    }

    int nKind = 1;
    string sKind = SubString(sTag, 4, 3);
    if (sKind == "arm")
    {
        nKind = 0;
    }
    else if (sKind != "wpn")
    {
        return -1;
    }

    int nElement = -1;
    string sElement = SubString(sTag, 8, 3);
    if (sElement == "fir") { nElement = 0; }
    else if (sElement == "ice") { nElement = 1; }
    else if (sElement == "lit") { nElement = 2; }
    else if (sElement == "nat") { nElement = 3; }
    else if (sElement == "spi") { nElement = 4; }
    if (nElement < 0)
    {
        return -1;
    }

    return (nKind * 25) + (nElement * 5) + StringToInt(SubString(sTag, 12, 2));
}
// Shale's crystals are the one thing here that is not a material: the quality is the
// template itself, so raising it means creating a better crystal and removing the old
// one. The name cannot be built at run time - CreateItemOnObject takes a resource and
// there is no conversion from a string - so every combination is spelled out.
object CreateCrystal(int nIndex, object oOwner)
{
    switch (nIndex)
    {
        case  0: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_fir_00.uti", oOwner);
        case  1: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_fir_01.uti", oOwner);
        case  2: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_fir_02.uti", oOwner);
        case  3: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_fir_03.uti", oOwner);
        case  4: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_fir_04.uti", oOwner);
        case  5: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_ice_00.uti", oOwner);
        case  6: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_ice_01.uti", oOwner);
        case  7: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_ice_02.uti", oOwner);
        case  8: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_ice_03.uti", oOwner);
        case  9: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_ice_04.uti", oOwner);
        case 10: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_lit_00.uti", oOwner);
        case 11: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_lit_01.uti", oOwner);
        case 12: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_lit_02.uti", oOwner);
        case 13: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_lit_03.uti", oOwner);
        case 14: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_lit_04.uti", oOwner);
        case 15: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_nat_00.uti", oOwner);
        case 16: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_nat_01.uti", oOwner);
        case 17: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_nat_02.uti", oOwner);
        case 18: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_nat_03.uti", oOwner);
        case 19: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_nat_04.uti", oOwner);
        case 20: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_spi_00.uti", oOwner);
        case 21: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_spi_01.uti", oOwner);
        case 22: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_spi_02.uti", oOwner);
        case 23: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_spi_03.uti", oOwner);
        case 24: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_arm_spi_04.uti", oOwner);
        case 25: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_fir_00.uti", oOwner);
        case 26: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_fir_01.uti", oOwner);
        case 27: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_fir_02.uti", oOwner);
        case 28: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_fir_03.uti", oOwner);
        case 29: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_fir_04.uti", oOwner);
        case 30: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_ice_00.uti", oOwner);
        case 31: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_ice_01.uti", oOwner);
        case 32: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_ice_02.uti", oOwner);
        case 33: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_ice_03.uti", oOwner);
        case 34: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_ice_04.uti", oOwner);
        case 35: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_lit_00.uti", oOwner);
        case 36: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_lit_01.uti", oOwner);
        case 37: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_lit_02.uti", oOwner);
        case 38: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_lit_03.uti", oOwner);
        case 39: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_lit_04.uti", oOwner);
        case 40: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_nat_00.uti", oOwner);
        case 41: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_nat_01.uti", oOwner);
        case 42: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_nat_02.uti", oOwner);
        case 43: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_nat_03.uti", oOwner);
        case 44: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_nat_04.uti", oOwner);
        case 45: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_spi_00.uti", oOwner);
        case 46: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_spi_01.uti", oOwner);
        case 47: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_spi_02.uti", oOwner);
        case 48: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_spi_03.uti", oOwner);
        case 49: return CreateItemOnObject(R"shl_wpn_spi_04.uti", oOwner);
    }
    return OBJECT_INVALID;
}

// Raises one crystal to the target quality. The replacement is created first so that a
// failed creation leaves the original in place, and it lands in its owner's inventory
// rather than in whatever slot the old one occupied.
int UpgradeCrystal(object oItem, int nTargetQuality, object oOwner)
{
    int nIndex = CrystalIndex(oItem);
    if (nIndex < 0)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    int nQuality = nIndex % 5;
    if (nQuality >= nTargetQuality)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    if (g_bDryRun == TRUE)
    {
        Record(GetName(oItem) + " " + IntToString(nQuality + 1) + "->" + IntToString(nTargetQuality + 1));
        g_nChanged = g_nChanged + 1;
        return 1;
    }

    int nFamily = nIndex - nQuality;
    object oNew = CreateCrystal(nFamily + nTargetQuality, oOwner);
    if (oNew == OBJECT_INVALID)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    Record(GetName(oNew) + " " + IntToString(nQuality + 1) + "->" + IntToString(nTargetQuality + 1));
    DestroyObject(oItem);
    g_nChanged = g_nChanged + 1;
    return 1;
}

int SweepCrystals(object oCreature, int nTargetQuality, int nOptions)
{
    int nChanged = 0;
    object[] aItems = GetItemsInInventory(oCreature, nOptions, 0, "", TRUE);
    int nCount = GetArraySize(aItems);
    int nIndex = 0;
    while (nIndex < nCount)
    {
        if (g_nChanged >= MAX_UPDATES)
        {
            break;
        }
        object oItem = aItems[nIndex];
        if (oItem != OBJECT_INVALID)
        {
            nChanged = nChanged + UpgradeCrystal(oItem, nTargetQuality, oCreature);
        }
        nIndex = nIndex + 1;
    }
    return nChanged;
}
// Raises one item to the best material its progression row offers within reach of the
// target tier. Returns 1 when the item changed.
int UpgradeItem(object oItem, int nTargetTier, int nIndex, int nCount)
{
    int nOld = GetItemMaterialType(oItem);

    // A named item is one the game never generates: it sits in a chest or is handed
    // over as a reward, at the material it was authored with, and no store ever rerolls
    // it. Its progression row only describes the family it belongs to - one row can cover
    // hundreds of templates - so following the row there invents tiers the piece was
    // never made in. The Helm of Honnleath is the case that showed this.
    if (GetItemUnique(oItem) == TRUE)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    // An item the game cannot name is not gear the player can equip, see or sell - it is
    // a creature's built-in attack. The Mabari's bite carries a real progression row and
    // sits in the party inventory, so without this it is raised to Dragonbone and a
    // companion silently hits harder than the designers authored.
    if (GetName(oItem) == "")
    {
        return 0;
    }

    // Only weapons, shields and armour are raised. The base item table (6) records what
    // each kind of item is, and everything that is not gear - rings, amulets, gifts,
    // quick items, quest trinkets - is ITEM_TYPE_MISC. A quest sword that sits in the
    // "Other" tab and can only be sold is one of those, so it is left alone.
    //
    // A table lookup that returns nothing is treated as unknown and allowed through. The
    // filter is worth having, but not at the cost of silently skipping gear if the table
    // or the column is named differently than expected.
    if (GetM2DAInt(TABLE_ITEMS, "Type", GetBaseItemType(oItem)) == ITEM_TYPE_MISC)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    // The item's own progression is the authority on what it may become: no row means
    // there is nothing to walk.
    int nProg = GetItemMaterialProgression(oItem);
    if (nProg <= 0)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    // The tier comes from the family ladders, which cover wood and leather as well as
    // metal; the row above decided which materials are permitted, and this decides how
    // good they are. Both are needed: the row without tiers skipped every bow, and
    // tiers without the row invented materials the item does not exist in.
    int nCurrentTier = TierOfMaterial(nOld);
    if (nCurrentTier <= 0)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    // Scan the whole row and keep the best material whose tier is within reach. The
    // row is the hard ceiling, so an item that stops at Steel stays at Steel however
    // generous the level is; the level only decides how far up the row we may go.
    int nBestTier = 0;
    int nBestMaterial = 0;
    int nCol = 1;
    while (nCol <= MAT_COLUMNS)
    {
        int nCandidate = MaterialAtColumn(nProg, nCol);
        if (nCandidate != 0)
        {
            int nCandidateTier = TierOfMaterial(nCandidate);
            if (nCandidateTier <= nTargetTier && nCandidateTier > nBestTier)
            {
                nBestTier = nCandidateTier;
                nBestMaterial = nCandidate;
            }
        }
        nCol = nCol + 1;
    }

    if (nBestMaterial == 0 || nBestTier <= nCurrentTier)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    if (g_nChanged >= MAX_UPDATES)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    // A dry run stops here: the item is reported but left alone, and the counter that
    // guards against a runaway loop still advances so the list stays bounded.
    if (g_bDryRun == TRUE)
    {
        g_nChanged = g_nChanged + 1;
        Record(GetName(oItem) + " " + IntToString(nCurrentTier)
            + "->" + IntToString(nBestTier));
        return 1;
    }

    // Apply the chosen material. The tier was already checked against the row and the
    // level, so there is no walk here; a rejected write only leaves the item as it was.
    SetItemMaterialType(oItem, nBestMaterial);
    if (GetItemMaterialType(oItem) == nBestMaterial)
    {
        g_nChanged = g_nChanged + 1;
        Record(GetName(oItem) + " " + IntToString(nCurrentTier)
            + "->" + IntToString(nBestTier));
        return 1;
    }
    SetItemMaterialType(oItem, nOld);
    return 0;
}

// Sweeps one inventory, one item at a time.
//
// The type filter is deliberately left at 0. GetItemsInInventory's third argument
// expects a BASE_ITEM_* value, which is a different enumeration from the ITEM_TYPE_*
// constants returned by GetItemType; passing those was selecting arbitrary base item
// types rather than weapons and armour, which is why whole categories of gear were
// never touched. The material ladder is the real filter: anything whose material has
// no tier is skipped for the cost of one call.
//
// The options are passed in because the party inventory is shared: reading it once with
// GET_ITEMS_OPTION_ALL covers everything the party owns, while each companion's worn gear
// is only reachable through GET_ITEMS_OPTION_EQUIPPED on that companion.
int SweepType(object oCreature, int nTargetTier, int nBaseItemType, int nOptions)
{
    int nChanged = 0;
    object[] aItems = GetItemsInInventory(oCreature, nOptions,
        nBaseItemType, "", TRUE);
    int nCount = GetArraySize(aItems);
    int nIndex = 0;
    int nRefreshes = 0;
    while (nIndex < nCount)
    {
        if (g_nChanged >= MAX_UPDATES)
        {
            break;
        }
        object oItem = aItems[nIndex];

        // Fetching the list again after every change costs a full inventory read each
        // time, which is far too much at this size. Nothing of the sort has ever been
        // needed: no dead reference has been observed. The list is therefore only
        // re-read if a dead reference actually turns up.
        if (oItem == OBJECT_INVALID)
        {
            nRefreshes = nRefreshes + 1;
            if (nRefreshes > 4)
            {
                break;
            }
            aItems = GetItemsInInventory(oCreature, nOptions,
                nBaseItemType, "", TRUE);
            nCount = GetArraySize(aItems);
            oItem = aItems[nIndex];
            if (oItem == OBJECT_INVALID)
            {
                nIndex = nIndex + 1;
                continue;
            }
        }

        nIndex = nIndex + 1;
        nChanged = nChanged + UpgradeItem(oItem, nTargetTier, nIndex, nCount);
    }
    return nChanged;
}

int UpgradeCreature(object oCreature, int nTargetTier, int nOptions)
{
    if (oCreature == OBJECT_INVALID)
    {
        return 0;
    }
    return SweepType(oCreature, nTargetTier, 0, nOptions);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- reporting

// Only floaties render in this build: the on-screen debug text and the GUI log
// window both produce nothing, and the script logging functions have no writable
// category (the executable has no ECLog support at all). Floaties do render after
// an item has been changed -- the existing UpgradeItems mod relies on exactly that
// -- so every change is reported as it happens, one floaty per item. A run that
// stops partway therefore ends on the name of the item it stopped at, and no state
// has to survive between invocations.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- entry point

void main()
{
    object oHero = GetHero();
    if (oHero == OBJECT_INVALID)
    {
        return;
    }

    int nTargetTier = 0;
    string sArgument = GetLocalString(GetModule(), "RUNSCRIPT_VAR");
    int nForced = StringToInt(sArgument);

    // The argument is the target tier, and a leading minus requests a dry run: the same
    // sweep and the same report, but nothing is written to the items. The minus is read
    // from the text rather than the parsed number so that "-" alone, or "-0", means "dry
    // run at the tier your level earns" instead of collapsing into the plain no-argument
    // case.
    if (SubString(sArgument, 0, 1) == "-")
    {
        g_bDryRun = TRUE;
        nForced = 0 - nForced;
    }
    if (nForced >= 1 && nForced <= MAX_TIER)
    {
        nTargetTier = nForced;
    }
    int nHeroLevel = CreatureLevel(oHero);
    if (nTargetTier == 0)
    {
        nTargetTier = TierForLevel(nHeroLevel);
    }

    // "c1" to "c5" switches to Shale's crystals, and to nothing else: they are a different
    // kind of upgrade, so mixing them into a material run would either do half a job or
    // destroy items the player did not ask about. The same leading minus gives a dry run.
    string sCrystal = sArgument;
    if (SubString(sCrystal, 0, 1) == "-")
    {
        sCrystal = SubString(sCrystal, 1, GetStringLength(sCrystal) - 1);
    }
    int nCrystalQuality = 0;
    if (SubString(sCrystal, 0, 1) == "c" || SubString(sCrystal, 0, 1) == "C")
    {
        nCrystalQuality = StringToInt(SubString(sCrystal, 1, 2));
        if (nCrystalQuality < 1)
        {
            nCrystalQuality = 1;
        }
        if (nCrystalQuality > 5)
        {
            nCrystalQuality = 5;
        }
    }

    g_nChanged = 0;
    g_sUpdated = "";

    if (nCrystalQuality > 0)
    {
        int nTarget = nCrystalQuality - 1;
        SweepCrystals(oHero, nTarget, GET_ITEMS_OPTION_ALL);

        object[] aCrystalParty = GetPartyList();
        int nCrystalCount = GetArraySize(aCrystalParty);
        int nCrystalIndex = 0;
        while (nCrystalIndex < nCrystalCount)
        {
            if (aCrystalParty[nCrystalIndex] != oHero)
            {
                SweepCrystals(aCrystalParty[nCrystalIndex], nTarget, GET_ITEMS_OPTION_EQUIPPED);
            }
            nCrystalIndex = nCrystalIndex + 1;
        }

        object[] aCrystalPool = GetPartyPoolList();
        nCrystalCount = GetArraySize(aCrystalPool);
        nCrystalIndex = 0;
        while (nCrystalIndex < nCrystalCount)
        {
            if (aCrystalPool[nCrystalIndex] != oHero)
            {
                SweepCrystals(aCrystalPool[nCrystalIndex], nTarget, GET_ITEMS_OPTION_EQUIPPED);
            }
            nCrystalIndex = nCrystalIndex + 1;
        }

        if (g_sUpdated == "")
        {
            DisplayFloatyMessage(oHero, "nothing to upgrade",
                FLOATY_MESSAGE, 16777215, 10.0);
        }
        else
        {
            DisplayFloatyMessage(oHero, BuildReport(g_sUpdated),
                FLOATY_MESSAGE, 16777215, 30.0);
        }
        return;
    }

    // The party inventory is one shared stash, so it is read exactly once. Reading it per
    // creature returned the same items over and over and reported each change once per
    // creature, which is where counts ten times too high came from. Worn gear is the only
    // thing that differs per creature, and GET_ITEMS_OPTION_EQUIPPED is what reaches it.
    UpgradeCreature(oHero, nTargetTier, GET_ITEMS_OPTION_ALL);

    object[] aParty = GetPartyList();
    int nParty = GetArraySize(aParty);
    int nIndex = 0;
    while (nIndex < nParty)
    {
        if (aParty[nIndex] != oHero)
        {
            UpgradeCreature(aParty[nIndex], nTargetTier, GET_ITEMS_OPTION_EQUIPPED);
        }
        nIndex = nIndex + 1;
    }

    // Companions left at camp are not in the party list, but they still have worn gear.
    object[] aPool = GetPartyPoolList();
    int nPool = GetArraySize(aPool);
    nIndex = 0;
    while (nIndex < nPool)
    {
        if (aPool[nIndex] != oHero)
        {
            UpgradeCreature(aPool[nIndex], nTargetTier, GET_ITEMS_OPTION_EQUIPPED);
        }
        nIndex = nIndex + 1;
    }

    // One floaty, one line per distinct item and tier change, prefixed with how many
    // of that item were affected. Nothing else is printed - a run with no changes says
    // so, because silence is otherwise indistinguishable from a script that did not run.
    if (g_sUpdated == "")
    {
        DisplayFloatyMessage(oHero, "nothing to upgrade",
            FLOATY_MESSAGE, 16777215, 5.0);
    }
    else
    {
        DisplayFloatyMessage(oHero, BuildReport(g_sUpdated),
            FLOATY_MESSAGE, 16777215, 5.0);
    }
}
